From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: host-common: let endpoints alone decide D3cold eligibility

pci_host_common_d3cold_possible() walks every device under the host
bridge and refuses D3cold unless each one sits in D3hot. On the AYN
Odin 3 (SM8750) the root port advertises hotplug capability in its slot
register, so it is flagged is_pciehp, and without CONFIG_HOTPLUG_PCI_PCIE
and pcie_port_pm=force pci_bridge_d3_possible() returns false. The port
is then not power-manageable, pci_pm_suspend_noirq() leaves it in D0,
the walk fails on the port itself, and dw_pcie_suspend_noirq() returns
early with the controller, PHY, link and clocks all kept on. The WiFi
endpoint had already reached D3hot; the veto was the root port.

Skip bridges in the walk. The DesignWare host tears the link down and
reinitialises the port on resume regardless of the port's PM state, so
the endpoint's state is what matters. This mirrors the endpoint-only
eligibility Armada validated on SM8550.

--- a/drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c
@@ -289,6 +289,16 @@
 	u32 *flags = userdata;
 
 	if (!pdev->dev.driver && !pci_is_enabled(pdev))
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * Only endpoints decide D3cold eligibility. A root port that
+	 * advertises hotplug capability is never bridge_d3 without native
+	 * PCIe hotplug and pcie_port_pm=force, so it stays in D0 through
+	 * system suspend and would veto the power-off of a host whose link
+	 * is torn down and reinitialised on resume anyway.
+	 */
+	if (pci_is_bridge(pdev))
 		return 0;
 
 	if (pdev->current_state != PCI_D3hot)
